  "strategy": "Our goal is to minimize J(a) = 2 * n * max(convolve(a, a)) / (sum(a))^2, with a being a nonnegative step function.\n\nWe propose a novel approach using a multi-start pattern projection method that combines global search with local optimization and asymmetric smoothing.\n\nKey steps:\n1. **Pattern Projection**: Start with a valid parent sequence (if available) and also generate diverse base sequences (smoothbumps, plateaus, random patterns) to explore different starting points.\n2. **Gradient-based Escaping**: For each base sequence, run a gradient-based optimization to escape local minima. This involves:\n   - Smooth max approximation by convolving with various frequencies\n   - Periodic restarts with structured asymmetric perturbations\n3. **Overlap Control**: Use a reduced-constraint LP approach to enforce overlap bounds without blocking potentially useful patterns\n4. **High-pass Filtering**: Combine local optimization with high-pass filtering to emphasize sharp transitions that might reduce J(a)\n5. **Data-driven Restarting**: Automatically restart when exceeding a grid density threshold or deviating from the target J(a)\n\nImplementation specifics:\n- Define a function to generate diverse base sequences (`generate_patterns`)\n- Use a grid of candidate estimates with varying step sizes\n- For each candidate, run a while loop until improvement stops\n- Compute dual_gap = max(conv) - (sum(a))^2/2n (rough minimum)\n- Each iteration updates:\n    a_prev = (1-t)*a + t*g (with t chosen to minimize J)\n    g solved by LP with minimal binding constraints\n- Special termination conditions when J becomes too large or too small\n\nThe algorithm balances solver complexity with the need to avoid local minima while respecting the time budget.\n\nSection breakdown:\n1. Approach and rationale\n2. Mathematical specification\n3. Implementation procedure for candidates\n4. Search choices and variations\n5. Budget control and termination checks\n6. Final validation and candidate return",
